### Academic Analysis

The computational efficiency of range queries reveals a stark contrast between linear and logarithmic time complexities. The Naive approach exhibits a point update complexity of $\Theta(1)$ and a range query complexity of $\Theta(N)$, leading to a total worst-case time complexity of $O(Q \cdot N)$. Conversely, the Segment Tree optimizes the query process by pre-calculating sums over hierarchical intervals. The construction of the tree is $\Theta(N)$, while both point updates and range queries are bounded by $\Theta(\log N)$. From a lower-bound perspective, any range sum query in a dynamic array must be $\Omega(1)$ in the best case (querying a single element) and $\Omega(\log N)$ in the worst case for any tree-based structure, as one must traverse the height of the tree to reconcile the root value.

The structural efficiency of the Segment Tree is rooted in its recurrence relations. The construction process follows $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + \Theta(1)$, which, by the Master Theorem, resolves to $\Theta(n)$. The traversal for updates or queries follows $T(n) = T(n/2) + \Theta(1)$, resulting in $\Theta(\log n)$. The theoretical bound of the tree height is proven by the fact that a binary tree containing $N$ leaves must have a minimum height $h$ such that $2^h \ge N$, which implies $h \ge \lceil \log_2 N \rceil$. Because the Segment Tree is a balanced complete binary tree, its height is strictly logarithmic, ensuring that any path from the root to a leaf is $O(\log N)$.

Despite the asymptotic superiority of the Segment Tree, physical hardware constraints introduce critical trade-offs. The Naive strategy utilizes a contiguous block of memory, maximizing spatial locality and benefiting from CPU L1/L2 cache prefetching. In contrast, a Segment Tree increases space complexity from $\Theta(N)$ to $\Theta(4N)$, introducing significant memory overhead. While the array-based implementation of the tree (using indices $2i$ and $2i+1$) is more cache-friendly than a pointer-based node structure, it still suffers from "pointer chasing" behavior across non-contiguous memory addresses during traversal. Thus, for very small $N$ or extremely infrequent queries, the constant factors associated with cache misses and memory overhead may render the naive approach unexpectedly competitive, despite the divergence in Big O notation.

